<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Schema for GSFIT_code_settings.xml, the file GSFit reads its settings from.

    GSFIT_code_settings.xml holds no settings itself. It links to the files which do, one element per
    file, with the file's path in `href`: database_reader.xml and database_writer.xml, which pick the
    database reader and writer for the whole run, then, inside `ids`, one file per IDS, each in its own
    directory.

    Conventions shared by every schema in this directory, and its sub-directories:
    * The schemas are laid out as a settings directory is, so the schema of a settings file has the same
      path, e.g. wall/wall.xsd for wall/wall.xml, and equilibrium/grid.xsd for equilibrium/grid.xml.
    * Each IDS has its own directory and file, named as in the IMAS Data Dictionary, e.g. wall/wall.xml,
      linked from `ids` in GSFIT_code_settings.xml.
      * The IDSs GSFit reads, e.g. wall, pf_active and magnetics, are built by the database reader, which
        `method` in database_reader.xml picks. Which run of which MDSplus tree to read, e.g. ELMAG's RUN16, is
        set once, in workflow.xml, the central list of the trees which the database reader reads from. Each
        IDS's file links to it, in `workflow`, e.g. `<workflow href="../workflow.xml"/>` in
        pf_active/pf_active.xml, and the reader selects the trees it needs from it by their `tree_name`. So far only the
        st40_mdsplus_with_digital_filter reader reads every IDS's file; the st40_mdsplus reader reads only
        thomson_scattering.xml. An IDS's file holds only its link to workflow.xml, and the settings which
        do not come from a tree, such as tf's reference radius, which every reader needs.
      * The IDS GSFit writes, equilibrium, is built by GSFit itself. equilibrium/equilibrium.xml links to
        GSFit's own settings, e.g. the grid and the constraints, in the same directory.
    * A link's path is relative to the file holding the link, so a settings directory can use a file
      from another directory, e.g. href="../default/wall/wall.xml".
    * A link names the schema of the file it links to in `xs:appinfo/schema`, relative to the schema
      holding the link, e.g. `<schema>wall/wall.xsd</schema>`. So a linked file is checked against the
      right schema wherever it is, e.g. href="../default/wall/wall.xml".
    * A linked file's root element is the link's path below the root element of the file holding the
      link, with `__` for `/`, e.g. `database_reader`, and `constraints__flux_loop` for
      `constraints/flux_loop` in equilibrium.xml, so that the files, and the Python classes generated
      from them, do not share names. The exception is an IDS's file, whose root element is the IDS's
      name followed by `_settings`, e.g. `wall_settings`. So its Python class, `WallSettings`, is not
      mistaken for the IDS, or for GSFit's object holding it, `gsfit_rs.Wall`.
    * The constraints are named as in the IMAS Data Dictionary, e.g. `b_field_pol_probe` and
      `diamagnetic_flux` as in the equilibrium IDS's time_slice[:]/constraints, and `rogowski_coil` as in
      the magnetics IDS. `isoflux` is not in the Data Dictionary, so keeps GSFit's name.
    * Each schema stands alone, with no `xs:include` or `xs:import`, because the XML Form Editor does not
      follow them. Types used by several schemas are repeated in each, and must be the same in each: when
      the settings are recorded in an IDS's code/parameters, each linked file is written in its link's
      place, and its schema is merged into the linking one, e.g. workflow.xsd into pf_active/pf_active.xsd.
    * Every element and enum value is described by an `xs:documentation`, which editors show on hover.
    * Units are in `xs:appinfo/units`, as the full names of SI units, such as metre, second, ampere and
      tesla, and dimensionless for a quantity with no dimensions. Counts have no units. This differs from
      the IMAS Data Dictionary, which uses the SI symbols, such as m, s, A and T, and 1 for dimensionless.
    * The "method" elements are enums. Each value names the sibling element that holds that method's
      settings. The exception is `method` in database_reader.xml, which has no sibling elements: the reader
      it picks reads each IDS's file, and the workflow.xml they link to.
    * Named types are UpperCamelCase, e.g. `FiniteDouble`, and elements are snake_case. The same kind of value
      has the same type in every schema, following the Rust side:
      * f64 -> `FiniteDouble`, which excludes NaN, INF and -INF, or `FinitePositiveDouble` or
        `FiniteNonNegativeDouble` where the sign is fixed, e.g. a radius, a tolerance or a weight
      * a list of f64 -> `FiniteDoubleList`, or `FiniteNonNegativeDoubleList` for weights and tensions
      * usize -> xs:nonNegativeInteger, or xs:positiveInteger where 0 is not allowed, or a type of its own
        for a larger minimum, e.g. `NumberOfGridPoints`, at least 2
      * a signed integer, e.g. a pulse number, where MDSplus gives 0 and -1 a meaning -> xs:integer
      * bool -> `TrueOrFalse`, which, unlike xs:boolean, does not accept 1 and 0
      * a name, e.g. of a sensor or a run -> `Name`, which has no whitespace; free text, e.g. a comment -> xs:string
    * JSON's `null` is `xsi:nil="true"` on an element declared `nillable="true"`.
    * An array of numbers is a space-separated list, e.g. "0.1 0.2 0.3", such as the times in
      equilibrium/timeslices.xml. A matrix is an element holding only
      a repeated `row`, one per row, each a list of numbers. It is read into a 2D numpy array, of shape
      [n_rows, n_columns]: no `row` is shape (0, 0), and an empty `row` or rows of different lengths are an error.
    * A list of named items, such as the constraints, is one element per item, with its name in `name`.
      The element is named as a single item, e.g. one `flux_loop` per flux loop.
    * Children are in `xs:all` groups, so their order does not matter, except in a list.
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" elementFormDefault="unqualified">

    <!-- ================================= Types ================================= -->

    <xs:simpleType name="XmlPath">
        <xs:annotation>
            <xs:documentation>
                Path of an XML file, relative to the file holding the link
            </xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
        <xs:restriction base="xs:anyURI">
            <xs:pattern value="\S+\.xml"/>
        </xs:restriction>
    </xs:simpleType>

    <xs:complexType name="Link">
        <xs:annotation>
            <xs:documentation>
                A link to the file holding these settings
            </xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
        <xs:attribute name="href" type="XmlPath" use="required">
            <xs:annotation>
                <xs:documentation>
                    Path of the linked file, relative to GSFIT_code_settings.xml, e.g. "wall/wall.xml" or "../default/wall/wall.xml"
                </xs:documentation>
            </xs:annotation>
        </xs:attribute>
    </xs:complexType>

    <xs:complexType name="GsfitCodeSettingsIds">
        <xs:annotation>
            <xs:documentation>
                Settings needed to build IDSs. These settings will be stored within the IDSs once built.
            </xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
        <xs:all>
            <!-- The IDS GSFit writes, built by GSFit -->
            <xs:element name="equilibrium" type="Link">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `equilibrium` IDS; e.g. `time_slices`, the numerical grid, ...
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>equilibrium/equilibrium.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>

            <!-- The IDSs GSFit reads, built by the database reader -->
            <xs:element name="magnetics" type="Link">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `magnetics` IDS; e.g. where to read data from.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>magnetics/magnetics.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
            <xs:element name="pf_active" type="Link">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `pf_active` IDS; e.g. where to read data from.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>pf_active/pf_active.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
            <xs:element name="pf_passive" type="Link">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `pf_passive` IDS; e.g. where to read data from.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>pf_passive/pf_passive.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
            <xs:element name="pulse_schedule" type="Link" minOccurs="0">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `pulse_schedule` IDS; e.g. where to read data from.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>pulse_schedule/pulse_schedule.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
            <xs:element name="tf" type="Link">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `tf` IDS; e.g. where to read data from, and reference radius for toroidal vacuum field.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>tf/tf.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
            <xs:element name="thomson_scattering" type="Link" minOccurs="0">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `thomson_scattering` IDS; e.g. where to read data from, and which time-sices/channels have trustworthy data.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>thomson_scattering/thomson_scattering.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
            <xs:element name="wall" type="Link">
                <xs:annotation>
                    <xs:documentation>
                        Settings needed to build `wall` IDS; e.g. where to read data from.
                    </xs:documentation>
                    <xs:appinfo>
                        <schema>wall/wall.xsd</schema>
                    </xs:appinfo>
                </xs:annotation>
            </xs:element>
        </xs:all>
    </xs:complexType>

    <!-- ================================= The settings file ================================= -->

    <xs:element name="gsfit_code_settings">
        <xs:annotation>
            <xs:documentation>
                GSFit's settings. This file holds no settings itself; instead it links to different *.xml's.
            </xs:documentation>
        </xs:annotation>
        <xs:complexType>
            <xs:all>
                <!-- The database reader and writer, for the whole run -->
                <xs:element name="database_reader" type="Link">
                    <xs:annotation>
                        <xs:documentation>
                            The Python `database_reader` to use for the whole run.
                        </xs:documentation>
                        <xs:appinfo>
                            <schema>database_reader.xsd</schema>
                        </xs:appinfo>
                    </xs:annotation>
                </xs:element>
                <xs:element name="database_writer" type="Link">
                    <xs:annotation>
                        <xs:documentation>
                            List of Python `database_writer` to write to after the run completes.
                        </xs:documentation>
                        <xs:appinfo>
                            <schema>database_writer.xsd</schema>
                        </xs:appinfo>
                    </xs:annotation>
                </xs:element>

                <!-- The IDSs -->
                <xs:element name="ids" type="GsfitCodeSettingsIds">
                    <xs:annotation>
                        <xs:documentation>
                            Settings needed to build IDSs.
                        </xs:documentation>
                    </xs:annotation>
                </xs:element>

                <!-- RTGSFit: only read by the rtgsfit_mdsplus database writer -->
                <xs:element name="rtgsfit_code_settings" type="Link" minOccurs="0">
                    <xs:annotation>
                        <xs:documentation>
                            RTGSFit's own settings, which the rtgsfit_mdsplus writer stores for it. Only needed with the rtgsfit_mdsplus writer
                        </xs:documentation>
                        <xs:appinfo>
                            <schema>rtgsfit_code_settings.xsd</schema>
                        </xs:appinfo>
                    </xs:annotation>
                </xs:element>
                <xs:element name="rtgsfit_initial_conditions" type="Link" minOccurs="0">
                    <xs:annotation>
                        <xs:documentation>
                            The equilibrium RTGSFit starts from, which the rtgsfit_mdsplus writer stores for it. Only needed with the rtgsfit_mdsplus writer
                        </xs:documentation>
                        <xs:appinfo>
                            <schema>rtgsfit_initial_conditions.xsd</schema>
                        </xs:appinfo>
                    </xs:annotation>
                </xs:element>
                <xs:element name="sensor_replacement" type="Link" minOccurs="0">
                    <xs:annotation>
                        <xs:documentation>
                            The constraints which RTGSFit makes from other sensors. Optional, and only read by the rtgsfit_mdsplus writer
                        </xs:documentation>
                        <xs:appinfo>
                            <schema>sensor_replacement.xsd</schema>
                        </xs:appinfo>
                    </xs:annotation>
                </xs:element>
            </xs:all>
        </xs:complexType>
    </xs:element>
</xs:schema>
